        "original_id": "481cc9fc-bea0-569e-b3ec-9be1178035f8",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Laws: Black Act\n- People: Robert Peel\n- Roles: Prime Minister of the United Kingdom, Home Secretary\n- Topics: criminal law reform\n\nFacts:\n- The Black Act was largely repealed on 8 July 1823 after an early 19th-century criminal law reform campaign.\n- Robert Peel introduced the 1823 reform bill that brought about the Black Act’s repeal.\n- Sir Robert Peel, 2nd Baronet, was born on 5 February 1788 and died on 2 July 1850.\n- Robert Peel was a British statesman and a member of the Conservative Party.\n- Robert Peel served as Home Secretary from 1822 to 1827 and again from 1828 to 1830.\n- Robert Peel served as Prime Minister of the United Kingdom from 1834 to 1835 and again from 1841 to 1846.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "4ba3ead0-5a75-5035-a419-19dd7b1af5ca",
        "text": "Page: Black Act. Sentence: The Black Act (9 Geo.\nPage: Black Act. Sentence: Following a criminal law reform campaign in the early 19th century, it was largely repealed on 8 July 1823, when a reform bill introduced by Robert Peel came into force.\nPage: Robert Peel. Sentence: Sir Robert Peel, 2nd Baronet, {'1': \", '2': \", '3': \", '4': \"} (5 February 17882 July 1850) was a British statesman and member of the Conservative Party who served twice as Prime Minister of the United Kingdom (1834–1835 and 1841–1846) and twice as Home Secretary (1822–1827 and 1828–1830).",
